Subject: [PATCH 1/3] 0047 the drill could never attach the book it drills
 (5863 passed)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three defects, all found by you running the thing rather than by me
reading it.

D31 — THE DRILL COULD NEVER GET A CARRY BOOK. _load_bot called
build_bot(attach_strategy=False) to keep the directional voter out. The
CARRY book is gated behind the same flag, so it returned a bot with
`carry is None` and the drill reported "BOOK_MODE is not carry" against a
config that plainly said carry. It would have failed identically on Fly,
where the preflight is the entire point. Now build_bot() with no flag, as
main() does: the config decides, and the carry branch returns before the
voter is constructed. An AST test refuses any attach_strategy= keyword on
that call — by AST and not by grep, because the comment explaining the bug
names the flag and a string search would flag the explanation.

D32 — A READ-ONLY TOOL MODIFIED A TRACKED FILE. STATE_DB_PATH defaults to
state/trading_state.db, which in a checkout is the committed FIXTURE, so the
preflight left it dirty and scripts/verify.sh then refused to write a
receipt ("FIXTURE DB MUTATED"). Run the preflight, then run the suite, and
the suite refuses. The drill now takes a scratch database outside the
repository unless STATE_DB_PATH names one — on Fly, fly.toml does, so the
real one is used there. The test fingerprints every file in state/ by
content before and after a preflight, WAL and shm included: a journal left
behind is a modification the next git status shows.

D33 — deploy.sh looked for `fly`; the installer lays down `flyctl`. So it
reinstalled flyctl on a machine that already had it and then called a
command that was not there. One resolved $FLY now, flyctl preferred, and a
test refuses any bare `fly ` call left in the executed part of the script.

And the message. "BOOK_MODE is not carry; there is no book to drill" was
true and useless. It now prints the three exports that fix it, and says why
none of them has a default.

Verified by running: the drill reaches `reachability` and stops there with
the proxy's 403, which is the correct answer from a Codespace and the stop
the machine on Fly will not have. The fixture database is untouched
afterwards.

