From 0fd3f52b32c8f09740a32fd28516e96f163ac71c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:42:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] 0048 the margin gate measured the wrong thing (5881
 passed)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

INVENTORY F4, open since 0038, is the only remaining item that loses the
POSITION rather than costing money slowly. The book guarded its short with

    positionIM / positionMM >= 2.0

and that ratio is a LEVERAGE / RISK-TIER quantity: Bybit tier 1 is 0.66%
over 0.33%, so it sits near 2.0 by construction and barely moves as the
price does. It is not headroom. A short being squeezed would not have
tripped it, and a liquidated perp leg leaves the client naked long in
exactly the market that just moved against them — the single failure this
book exists to prevent.

WHAT THE VENUE ACTUALLY PUBLISHES. /v5/position/list returns liqPrice, the
estimated liquidation price, and /v5/account/wallet-balance returns
accountMMRate, which is what UTA cross margin liquidates on. Distance from
mark to liqPrice is a number a human can read: the price can move X% against
this before it is gone. Both are read, and the book leaves on the worse.

liqPrice is "" when it lies outside the venue's price bounds. That means
there is no REACHABLE liquidation price — the safest state there is — and it
comes back as None with a reason rather than as a zero that would read as
"liquidation is at zero" or get arithmetic done to it.

THE FLOOR IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN. Over the 4,500-settlement Bybit corpus,
the worst moves against a short:

    8h close to close       8.00%
    intra-settlement high  11.52%   <- what a liquidation engine sees
    24h                    17.93%

MIN_LIQUIDATION_DISTANCE_PCT = 15.0 clears the first two, and the docstring
carries the measurement; a test asserts the numbers sit next to the
constant. It is not tuned to make anything pass: a correctly collateralised
overlay — one BTC short against one BTC held — has no reachable liquidation
price at all, so this gate should never bind, and when it binds something
has gone wrong that a human needs to see.

WHY IT IS A HOLD-TIME CHECK. Before the book opens there is no position, so
the venue has no liquidation price to report. This cannot be an entry gate,
and the exposure F4 describes is exactly the held one.

NOTHING IS WEAKENED. The IM/MM floor is untouched and still runs; a position
must satisfy both. A test asserts the old floor still unwinds on its own.

An unreadable liquidation price HALTS — the same position _margin_multiple
has taken since 0023: a margin call you cannot see.

require_liquidation_check is OFF in the constructor so no existing caller or
test double changes behaviour, and ON in build_bot. That default is
disclosed here and asserted by a test, because the live book is the one that
must not be allowed to skip it.

STILL OPEN AT THE VENUE: no liqPrice has ever been read from a real
position, and whether Bybit liquidates precisely at accountMMRate 1.0 is
documented nowhere exact — 0.50 is half that distance and the book does not
wait to find out. Phase D.

